abstract:
The first unsupervised algorithm: no labels, only structure. K-Means alternates two steps — assign each point to its nearest centroid, move each centroid to its cluster's mean — and provably decreases the within-cluster sum of squares every time. We prove the convergence, explain why the mean is the optimal centre, work through the elbow method and silhouette score for choosing k, show k-means++ initialisation, and map the four ways K-Means fails.}
